# EXPECT: does not provably precede
#
# A function assigns a module name whose first TOP-LEVEL binding comes after
# a top-level statement that mentions the function — here a dead `if` arm.
# The VM runs this fine (rc 0, prints 7): the guarded call never executes,
# so by the time setit runs, module `v` exists and the write goes outward.
#
# The AOT's outward-write rule is static and conservative: the VM's real
# rule is RUNTIME (`is` updates an outer binding only if it exists AT THAT
# MOMENT, else it creates a function-local that dies with the call), and
# g_first_bind/g_first_use can only compare top-level statement order. A
# use it cannot prove safe is refused rather than answered — the arm's
# order-blind first version MANUFACTURED the module binding and turned a
# both-sides-die program into one that printed, rc 0.
#
# (The truly-broken variant — module `v` never bound at all — also refuses,
# but the VM dies rc 1 on it, so it cannot live in this tier: refuse
# fixtures test the envelope, programs the VM RUNS and the AOT declines.)
#
# The final `v is "s"` is LOAD-BEARING (verified by removing it): it demotes
# `v` from a clean numeric global, and the outward arm only guards DEMOTED
# module names — a clean numeric global takes the ordinary global path,
# which is order-correct, and this same program without that line compiles
# and matches.

define setit(k) as:
    v is k
    return 0

if 1 == 2:
    x is setit of 5

v is 0
x is setit of 7
print of v
v is "s"
print of v
